// TERMINAL VELOCITY — GOLDEN HOUR — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// TERMINAL VELOCITY as city pop — a game alternate of neon, selectable from the dev menu
// (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// neon-1 opens at golden hour, and this is what Tokyo sounds like then: the Seseragi
// figure on the neon cabinet's alloy chime with a CP-70 electric grand in unison, a
// DX bass, a gated snare, Rhodes comping, a clav cutting the off-beats and warm
// strings, at 120 with a light swing. Re-harmonised the way the genre would: every
// note of the figure is a chord tone of | C6/9 | Fmaj9 | Em11 | G13 |, the cadence is a
// ii–V–I, and the Shibuya melody turns out to spell minor-major ninths exactly.
// 
// THE TURN IS BEAT 56. The pre-chorus borrows A♭maj9 and B♭13 from the minor as the
// sun goes down; bar 14 stops dead after beat two while the chime sweeps down alone;
// and on the downbeat of bar 15 — NEON_MINOR_TURN_BEAT, neon-1's strike and its
// day-to-night change — the band hits the night chorus in C minor. The key change to
// D minor lands on bar 45, beat 176, neon's second strike.
// 
// The cabinet screen loops bars 5–8, and the level picks the song up where the screen
// leaves it — so the strike lands 6–9 bars (12–18 s) into neon-1, TERMINAL VELOCITY's
// own lead time. That matters: a train whose flight starts before the turn only flies
// past, and neon-1's first boardable train (progress 0.30, about 26 s in) must find the
// night already fallen. It is also why this is 120 and not slower.
// 
//   1–4    intro — the chime over Rhodes chords
//   5–10   verse — the band; the whole Seseragi phrase, C6/9 Fmaj9 Em11 G13, ii–V–I
//          (the cabinet screen plays 1–4 once and loops the verse's first four bars)
//   11–14  pre — A♭maj9 B♭13 A♭maj9 G7sus; stop after bar 14 beat 2, the sweep
//   15–20  NIGHT CHORUS (beat 56) — C minor: Cm9 A♭maj9 G7♭9 G7♭9 Fm9 A♭–B♭
//   21–28  chorus two — the second theme, brass stabs, Tron sixteenths under the bass
//   29–36  bridge — the Shibuya melody on a neon reed, cross-stick, walking bass
//   37–40  FALSE ENDING — one big C major hit, then the chime alone plays the day
//   41–44  build — B♭maj9 C13 B♭maj9 A7, the drums back, the riser
//   45–58  LAST CHORUS (beat 176) — D minor: the figure and the second theme, brass
//          throughout, celeste an octave over the chime
//   59–60  tag — B♭maj9, A♭maj9 and the minor sweep; the loop returns to bar 15
// 
// The melody is TERMINAL VELOCITY's own, note for note: the Seseragi figure major and
// minor with its cadence, the second theme (neon bars 29–36), the Shibuya melody and
// neon's minor sweep. Seseragi and the Shibuya melody are JR East's — the clearing
// caveat on neon.js carries over to this file unchanged.
// 
// 60 bars at 120, swing 56. Written by work/local/_neon-remix-golden-hour.mjs
// through work/local/_remix-lib.mjs; re-running it rewrites this file.
